2.64038 0. 32 0.26403 0.(Incremental software development is the process of building new programs by)awidthshow
289 85 gm
1.26724 0. 32 0.12672 0.(modifying old ones. This method of programming is extremely common, and takes)awidthshow
301 85 gm
0.53802 0. 32 0.05380 0.(advantage of existing pieces of software which are known to be correct.  The result is)awidthshow
313 85 gm
1.35589 0. 32 0.13558 0.(that there are many software versions in existence at the same time. To date, most)awidthshow
325 85 gm
1.07284 0. 32 0.10728 0.(software development tools have addressed only one aspect of incremental software)awidthshow
337 85 gm
0.68756 0. 32 0.06875 0.(development, namely, source code control. An equally important issue is in verifying)awidthshow
349 85 gm
1.16729 0. 32 0.11672 0.(that future versions of a program have the same dynamic behaviour as the original.)awidthshow
361 85 gm
0.96786 0. 32 0.09678 0.(Currently, there are no tools which address this issue of the equivalence of dynamic)awidthshow
373 85 gm
(program behaviour.)show
397 85 gm
1.03317 0. 32 0.10331 0.(In a number of papers [1, 2, 3, 4] we have introduced a new concept called relative)awidthshow
409 85 gm
2.46307 0. 32 0.24630 0.(debugging, which supports the incremental development of computer programs.)awidthshow
421 85 gm
0.62667 0. 32 0.06266 0.(Relative debugging makes it possible to compare the execution of two programs with)awidthshow
433 85 gm
0.96221 0. 32 0.09622 0.(the same functionality. The programs can run on the same computer, or on different)awidthshow
445 85 gm
1.89819 0. 32 0.18981 0.(computers connected by a network. They can differ in their source programming)awidthshow
457 85 gm
2.24731 0. 32 0.22473 0.(language or in implementation details. One of the programs usually serves as a)awidthshow
469 85 gm
-0.00386 0.(reference version which produces the desired results. The execution of the other process)ashow
481 85 gm
(is compared to the reference version in order to find discrepancies.)show
505 85 gm
0.21804 0. 32 0.02180 0.(This user manual describes the commands available in Guard. The reader is refereed to)awidthshow
517 85 gm
(the following papers for a more detailed discussion of the concepts in Guard.)show
541 85 gm
([1])show
541 113 gm
1.63223 0. 32 0.16322 0.(Abramson, D.A. and Sosic, R. "A Debugging Tool for Software Evolution",)awidthshow
